  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM MDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nShowers continue to subside over the northern half of the\nforecast area early this morning, with isolated thunderstorms\nover far western portions. This precipitation will end across\nthe area by 15Z this morning as winds begin to increase. A dry\nslot will be in place over the Cowboy State by this time, with\nthe main sfc low in the vicinity of MT/ND and the associated\noccluded low over western MT. West-southwest winds, with gusts\nof 25 to 35 mph will be in place from Kemmerer to Casper by this\ntime. Higher winds of 30 to 45 mph will occur over portions of \nYNP and the east slopes of the Absaroka, Bighorn and Wind River \nMountains. Winds will continue to increase across the forecast \narea, with gusts of 25 to 40 mph occurring for most areas by \n18Z. Fire weather will once again be a concern as a result, with\nnear- critical RH values returning. This will be especially \ntrue east of the Divide, where critical RHs will be in place in \nthe Bighorn Basin. A Red Flag Warning is in effect for Big Horn,\nWashakie and Hot Springs counties this afternoon as a result. A\ncold front will move eastward through the afternoon, exiting \nthe CWA into southeast WY between 06Z and 09Z Tuesday. Winds \nwill subside through the evening, becoming light for much of the\narea after midnight. Freezing temperatures will be possible in \nthe western valleys and portions of the Upper Green River Basin \nTuesday morning in the wake of the cold front. \n\nMore seasonal temperatures and dry conditions will remain in \nplace Tuesday, with freezing temperatures possible again in the \nwestern valleys and Upper Green River Basin Tuesday \nnight/Wednesday morning. Needless to say, sub-freezing \ntemperatures will be in place in the higher elevations the next \ntwo nights. Fire weather will be the main concern through the\nrest of the week. West-southwest winds, with gusts of 25 to 35\nmph, will occur each afternoon with slightly stronger gusts\npossible Thursday and Friday. Critical RHs will be widespread\neast of the Divide Tuesday, spreading across southern portions\nWednesday. An extended period of Red Flag Warnings looks likely. \n\nModels continue to trend toward a strong cold front impacting\nthe Cowboy State next weekend. The GFS continues to be the most\naggressive, digging a Canadian low southward to northern\nportions of the Great Basin and spreading widespread\nprecipitation over the state ahead of it. The ECMWF is slower\nand not as progressive, keeping the system over the Northern\nRockies and moving over the region Sunday into Monday next week.\nAlthough the timing remains influx, this continues to trend\ntoward the first taste of autumn to the region.",
